course: dictionary
lesson: 1466
title: Casual: relaxed and informal
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Casual means relaxed and informal, not stiff or formal. Casual clothes are comfy everyday clothes, not a suit or a uniform. The words around the word will show you which meaning is right.
explain: «He gave a casual shrug, but his heart was pounding.» The shrug looks relaxed, but the pounding heart tells you he was nervous. So casual means he is trying hard to look as if he does not care.
explain: Casual words belong in casual talk. «AHA is a casual yeah or uh-huh between friends; to a granny tak is better, because AHA can sound lazy.» Before you choose a casual word, ask yourself who you are talking to.
explain: Casual can also mean happening by chance, or not planned and not permanent. A casual meeting is a chance meeting, and a casual job is done only now and then. Check the clues to find the right meaning.
fact: Casual means relaxed and informal, not smart or formal.
fact: Casual clothes are comfortable everyday clothes, such as jeans and a T-shirt.
fact: «Smart casual» is a dress code: not a suit, but not scruffy either.
fact: «Casual» can mean happening by chance, or not regular, as in «a casual job».
fact: «zdorOv» is a very casual hiya for close friends, and «nE-a» is a casual nope between kids; both can sound rude to adults.
exercise: «He gave a casual shrug, but his heart was pounding.» What does casual suggest here? | *Relaxed, while trying not to show he is nervous | Very angry and loud | Happy to be in a hurry | Dressed in a smart suit
exercise: Mum says, «Smart casual, please» for the party. What kind of clothes does she mean? | Pyjamas | A swimming costume | *Neat and comfortable, but not a suit | A school uniform with a tie
exercise: Which word is a casual yeah between friends? | nE-a | lǎowài | *AHA | zdorOv
test: Ella says she has a casual job. What does casual job mean? | *Done now and then, not regular or permanent | A job only grown-ups are allowed to do | A job that needs a uniform | Regular and permanent, every day of the year
test: «Filler words like um, like and you know are fine in casual talk.» Where are filler words fine? | Nowhere at all | In a written exam answer | *In relaxed talk with friends | In a formal speech to the whole school
exam: Which sentence uses casual to mean relaxed and informal? | Lee wore a casual meeting in the hall | Casual is the name of a school trip | *Lee made a casual remark, as if it was no big deal | The casual job was on the moon
